      "text": "To determine the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to find when \\( f(x) = -2 \\) has a real solution \\( x \\). This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation of the form \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). For this quadratic equation to have real solutions, its discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\) into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real solutions, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\), we get:\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 4, we find:\n\n\\[\nc \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
