      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that \\( f(x) = -2 \\) has at least one real solution. This means the equation \\( x^2 + 3x + c = -2 \\) or equivalently \\( x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0 \\) should have real roots.\n\nLet's denote the quadratic equation by:\n\\[ x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0. \\]\n\nFor this quadratic equation to have real roots, its discriminant must be non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac. \\]\n\nIn our equation, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = c + 2 \\). Therefore, the discriminant is:\n\\[ \\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2). \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4(c + 2). \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 9 - 4c - 8. \\]\n\\[ \\Delta = 1 - 4c. \\]\n\nFor the quadratic equation to have real roots, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\\[ \\Delta \\geq 0. \\]\n\\[ 1 - 4c \\geq 0. \\]\n\\[ 1 \\geq 4c. \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\geq c. \\]\n\\[ c \\leq \\frac{1}{4}. \\]\n\nThe largest value of \\( c \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( c = \\frac{1}{4} \\). Therefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
